        -:  101:    // To emulate lexicographical_compare behavior we have to perform two comparisons - the forward and reverse one.
        -:  102:    // Then we know which bytes are equivalent and which ones are different, and for those different the comparison results
        -:  103:    // will be opposite. Then we'll be able to find the first differing comparison result (for both forward and reverse ways),
        -:  104:    // and depending on which way it is for, this will be the result of the operation. There are a few notes to consider:
        -:  105:    //
        -:  106:    // 1. Due to little endian byte order the first bytes go into the lower part of the xmm registers,
        -:  107:    //    so the comparison results in the least significant bits will actually be the most signigicant for the final operation result.
        -:  108:    //    This means we have to determine which of the comparison results have the least significant bit on, and this is achieved with
        -:  109:    //    the "(x - 1) ^ x" trick.
        -:  110:    // 2. Because there is only signed comparison in SSE/AVX, we have to invert byte comparison results whenever signs of the corresponding
        -:  111:    //    bytes are different. I.e. in signed comparison it's -1 < 1, but in unsigned it is the opposite (255 > 1). To do that we XOR left and right,
        -:  112:    //    making the most significant bit of each byte 1 if the signs are different, and later apply this mask with another XOR to the comparison results.
        -:  113:    // 3. pcmpgtw compares for "greater" relation, so we swap the arguments to get what we need.
